The Sound Blaster's DSP reset must not fabricate an interrupt, and the
silence block's must be one the guest can acknowledge.

Three ways `sb16` asserts IRQ 5 with nothing behind it that a driver can
clear. The card holds its line until the DSP status port is read (that is
the hardware, and QEMU models it); an assertion with no bit set in mixer
register 0x82 therefore holds the line for good, and on the edge-triggered
ISA PIC every later block completion is then a level-1 into an already-high
line -- no edge, no interrupt, the card is deaf until the next DSP reset.

  * `reset()` pulsed the line when auto-init DMA was running: a raise and
    an immediate lower, an interrupt no hardware generates and no status
    bit accompanies. On an edge-triggered i8259 the pulse latches IRR
    while the guest, which is resetting the DSP precisely because it is
    *finished*, has IRQ 5 masked -- so the bit stays pending, unowned and
    unacknowledgeable. Windows' VPICD will not unmask a physical IRQ in
    that state, and the next program to want the card never sees an
    interrupt at all.
  * `aux_timer()` (the end of a silence block, DSP command 0x80) raised
    the line without setting the status bit, so the driver's `in 0x2xE`
    could not clear it.
  * command 0x80's short-block path raised it inline, the same way.

Found through Duke Nukem 3D's SETUP.EXE on a Windows 98 guest: its "Test
Sound FX Card" plays once and every attempt after it fails with "Playback
failed, possibly due to an invalid or conflicting IRQ", because the DSP
reset ending the first test leaves IRQ 5 latched in the master PIC
(`info pic`: `pic0 irr=20 imr=b8`) and the second test's DMA blocks then
raise a line that is already high. The card itself has nothing pending
across it -- mixer 0x82 reads 0x00 -- which is what says the interrupt was
never the card's.

A DSP reset clears the pending interrupt and drops the line, and cancels a
silence block that has not expired: without that, the timer fires after the
reset and asserts the line behind the guest's back.

diff --git a/hw/audio/sb16.c b/hw/audio/sb16.c
index 1838d3ef7b..8d1de37125 100644
--- a/hw/audio/sb16.c
+++ b/hw/audio/sb16.c
@@ -197,6 +197,11 @@ static void aux_timer (void *opaque)
 {
     SB16State *s = opaque;
     s->can_write = 1;
+    /* The silence block is over, and its end is an ordinary 8-bit DSP
+       interrupt: the status bit has to be set or the driver's read of
+       the DSP status port will not acknowledge it and the line stays
+       asserted for good. */
+    s->mixer_regs[0x82] |= 1;
     qemu_irq_raise (s->pic);
 }
 
@@ -800,6 +805,7 @@ static void complete (SB16State *s)
                 bytes = samples << s->fmt_stereo << (s->fmt_bits == 16);
                 ticks = muldiv64(bytes, NANOSECONDS_PER_SECOND, freq);
                 if (ticks < NANOSECONDS_PER_SECOND / 1024) {
+                    s->mixer_regs[0x82] |= 1;
                     qemu_irq_raise (s->pic);
                 }
                 else {
@@ -894,11 +900,13 @@ static void legacy_reset (SB16State *s)
 
 static void reset (SB16State *s)
 {
-    qemu_irq_lower (s->pic);
-    if (s->dma_auto) {
-        qemu_irq_raise (s->pic);
-        qemu_irq_lower (s->pic);
+    /* A DSP reset clears any pending interrupt and drops the line; it
+       does not raise one, and a silence block that has not expired yet
+       must not raise one behind the guest's back either. */
+    if (s->aux_ts) {
+        timer_del (s->aux_ts);
     }
+    qemu_irq_lower (s->pic);
 
     s->mixer_regs[0x82] = 0;
     s->dma_auto = 0;
